\section{Specifying a column}

Most listings need nothing from a column but its title, so a plain
string must be an acceptable column specification; anything more
ceremonious would make the common case worse than the [[print]] call it
replaces.
The richer cases---a right-aligned count, a dimmed identifier, a free-text
field that must wrap rather than be truncated---need a few presentation
hints, which we collect in a small record.

Every default below is deliberately the same value
[[rich.table.Table.add_column]] already defaults to.
That gives us the invariant that makes mixing the two forms safe:
[[Column("Path")]] and the bare string [["Path"]] produce exactly the
same column, so a caller can upgrade one column of a listing to a
[[Column]] without perturbing the others.

The one field that is not a straight pass-through is [[fold]].
Rich spells this as [[overflow="fold"]], one string out of an
enumeration whose other values ([["ellipsis"]], [["crop"]],
[["ignore"]]) nytid never wants: a listing either lets a long cell wrap
onto further lines or leaves it to be truncated.  Since that is a
two-way choice we expose it as a boolean, which cannot be misspelled
into a silently wrong value the way a free-form string can.

<<the column specification>>=
@dataclass
class Column:
    """Presentation hints for one column of a listing.

    Only `header` is required.  `justify`, `style` and `no_wrap` are
    passed straight to `rich.table.Table.add_column`, and `fold` selects
    its `overflow="fold"` mode.  All of them are ignored when the output
    is piped, where the rows are printed as plain tab-separated text.
    """

    header: str
    justify: str = "left"
    style: str | None = None
    no_wrap: bool = False
    fold: bool = False
<<imports>>=
from dataclasses import dataclass
@

\section{The [[print_rows]] function}

The rows a caller passes are the \emph{piped} truth: one tuple per
line, one string per field, joined with tabs and printed verbatim.
Building the terminal table from those same tuples is what keeps the
two forms from drifting apart, and it is why the function takes rows
rather than objects---any formatting decision has already been made by
the caller and is therefore visible in both outputs.

That is exactly the property [[tty_rows]] deliberately breaks, for the
cases where it should not hold.
Some cells read better on screen after a substitution that would be
destructive in a pipe: a placeholder where a value is missing, a
relative \enquote{3 days ago} in place of a timestamp.
An empty field is \emph{information} to [[cut -f3]]---it keeps the
column count stable---but looks like a bug to a reader.
A caller that needs such a substitution passes a second row list used
only on the terminal.
The alternative, letting each command test [[sys.stdout.isatty()]]
itself and format accordingly, would reintroduce precisely the
duplication this module exists to remove.

We check [[rows]] rather than [[tty_rows]] for emptiness even on the
terminal path: the piped rows are the authoritative account of whether
there is anything to report, and a caller's display substitutions
should not be able to conjure a table out of no data.

<<the [[print_rows]] function>>=
def print_rows(columns, rows, tty_rows=None):
    """Print `rows` under `columns`, adapting to the output stream.

    Each element of `columns` is either a header string or a `Column`.
    Each element of `rows` is a tuple of strings with one entry per
    column.

    When stdout is a terminal the rows are printed as an aligned table
    with a header row; otherwise they are printed one tab-separated
    line per row, with no header, so that shell tools can consume them
    directly.  A single-column listing therefore degenerates to one
    bare value per line.

    `tty_rows`, if given, replaces `rows` on the terminal only, so that
    display-only substitutions never reach a pipeline.

    Nothing at all is printed when `rows` is empty.
    """
    if not rows:
        return
    if sys.stdout.isatty():
        <<render an aligned table on the terminal>>
    else:
        <<write the rows as headerless TSV>>
@

The piped form is the whole of the compatibility contract, so it is
also the whole of the implementation: no quoting, no escaping, no
header, nothing that a future change could accidentally add a byte to.

<<write the rows as headerless TSV>>=
for row in rows:
    print("\t".join(row))
@

Rich is imported inside the branch rather than at module scope, which
is the pattern [[track ls]] and [[track stats]] established: the import
pulls in a sizeable dependency tree, and the overwhelmingly common
case---a command in a pipe or a shell completion---never reaches this
code.
Since every listing in nytid now routes through here, that saving
applies to all of them at once.

The lazy import has a second, unplanned benefit, which the tests below
rely on: because the name [[Console]] is resolved from [[rich.console]]
at call time, a test can substitute a console writing to a buffer of
known width without this function having to grow an injection
parameter for it.

<<render an aligned table on the terminal>>=
from rich.console import Console
from rich import box
from rich.table import Table

console = Console()
table = Table(show_header=True, header_style="bold",
              box=box.SIMPLE, show_edge=False,
              pad_edge=False)
for column in columns:
    <<add [[column]] to [[table]]>>
for row in (rows if tty_rows is None else tty_rows):
    table.add_row(*row)
console.print(table)
@

Normalising a bare string into a [[Column]] here, at the single point
of use, is what lets the two spellings coexist without the rest of the
function knowing which one it was given.

<<add [[column]] to [[table]]>>=
if isinstance(column, str):
    column = Column(column)
table.add_column(column.header,
                 justify=column.justify,
                 style=column.style,
                 no_wrap=column.no_wrap,
                 overflow="fold" if column.fold else "ellipsis")
@

\section{Testing the two forms}

The tests are tangled into [[tests/test_cliutilsoutput.py]].
The piped form is checked against exact strings, because its exact
bytes are the contract that shell scripts depend on; under [[pytest]]
stdout is not a terminal, so those tests need no setup beyond
[[capsys]].

<<test [[cliutilsoutput.py]]>>=
import io
import sys

sys.path.insert(0, "../src")

import pytest
import rich.console
import rich.table

from nytid.cli.utils.output import Column, print_rows

<<test helpers>>

<<test functions>>
@

Reaching the terminal branch takes two substitutions, and the second
is only possible because the branch imports rich lazily.
We replace stdout with a buffer that claims to be a terminal, which
selects the branch, and we replace [[rich.console.Console]] with one
bound to that same buffer at a fixed width.
Pinning the width matters: rich otherwise measures the real terminal
(via the standard file descriptors, not via the file it was handed),
which would make the rendered layout depend on the window the test
suite happens to run in.
Asking for a non-terminal console keeps colour escapes out of the
captured text, so the assertions can be about content rather than
about ANSI sequences.

The natural home for that setup would be a fixture, but it cannot go
there: pytest's own capture plugin reinstalls [[sys.stdout]] when it
resumes capturing for the call phase, \emph{after} fixture setup has
finished.
A patch applied in a fixture is therefore silently undone before the
test body runs---the symptom is a test that quietly exercises the
piped branch and fails on rendering assertions with tab-separated text
in its captured output.
So the helper is an ordinary function that patches and then calls
[[print_rows]] itself, keeping both inside the call phase.

<<test helpers>>=
class TTYBuffer(io.StringIO):
  """A stdout stand-in that captures text but claims to be a TTY."""

  def isatty(self):
    return True


def render_on_tty(monkeypatch, *args, **kwargs):
  """Return what print_rows(*args) renders with stdout a terminal."""
  buffer = TTYBuffer()
  real_console = rich.console.Console
  monkeypatch.setattr(sys, "stdout", buffer)
  monkeypatch.setattr(
    rich.console, "Console",
    lambda: real_console(file=buffer, width=80,
                         force_terminal=False))
  print_rows(*args, **kwargs)
  return buffer.getvalue()
@

A piped multi-column listing is tab-separated and carries no header:
<<test functions>>=
def test_print_rows_pipes_headerless_tsv(capsys):
  print_rows(["Register", "Path"],
             [("mine", "/tmp/mine"),
              ("shared", "/tmp/shared")])
  assert capsys.readouterr().out == (
    "mine\t/tmp/mine\n"
    "shared\t/tmp/shared\n")
@

The single-column case is the one shell loops depend on, so we pin it
separately: with one column the tab join has nothing to join and each
line is a bare value, exactly what [[print(value)]] used to produce.
<<test functions>>=
def test_print_rows_single_column_pipes_bare_lines(capsys):
  print_rows(["Course"], [("DD1310",), ("DD1321",)])
  assert capsys.readouterr().out == "DD1310\nDD1321\n"
@

Display substitutions must not leak into a pipeline: an empty field
stays empty there, however it is rendered on screen.
<<test functions>>=
def test_print_rows_ignores_tty_rows_when_piped(capsys):
  print_rows(["Deadline"], [("",)], tty_rows=[("(none)",)])
  assert capsys.readouterr().out == "\n"
@

Silence on an empty listing has to hold on both paths, since a header
with nothing under it is as unhelpful on screen as it is corrupting in
a pipe:
<<test functions>>=
def test_print_rows_pipes_nothing_without_rows(capsys):
  print_rows(["Course"], [])
  assert capsys.readouterr().out == ""


def test_print_rows_prints_nothing_without_rows_on_tty(monkeypatch):
  assert render_on_tty(monkeypatch, ["Course"], []) == ""
@

On a terminal the same data comes back as a table: the headers appear,
and the tab that a pipeline would rely on does not.
<<test functions>>=
def test_print_rows_renders_a_table_on_a_tty(monkeypatch):
  rendered = render_on_tty(
    monkeypatch, ["Register", "Path"], [("mine", "/tmp/mine")])
  assert "Register" in rendered
  assert "Path" in rendered
  assert "mine" in rendered
  assert "\t" not in rendered
@

And there, and only there, [[tty_rows]] takes over:
<<test functions>>=
def test_print_rows_uses_tty_rows_on_a_tty(monkeypatch):
  rendered = render_on_tty(
    monkeypatch, ["Deadline"], [("",)], tty_rows=[("(none)",)])
  assert "(none)" in rendered
@

Finally we pin the column specification against rich's own interface
rather than against rendered pixels, which would only tell us that
\emph{something} was aligned.
Recording the [[add_column]] calls also lets us assert the claim made
above that a bare string is indistinguishable from a defaulted
[[Column]]---the two entries below carry identical keyword arguments.
<<test functions>>=
def test_column_spec_reaches_rich(monkeypatch):
  recorded = []
  real_table = rich.table.Table

  class RecordingTable(real_table):
    def add_column(self, header, **kwargs):
      recorded.append((header, kwargs))
      super().add_column(header, **kwargs)

  monkeypatch.setattr(rich.table, "Table", RecordingTable)
  render_on_tty(
    monkeypatch,
    [Column("ID", justify="right", style="cyan", no_wrap=True),
     Column("Labels", fold=True),
     "Who"],
    [("7", "a,b", "dbosk")])

  assert recorded[0] == ("ID", {"justify": "right",
                                "style": "cyan",
                                "no_wrap": True,
                                "overflow": "ellipsis"})
  assert recorded[1] == ("Labels", {"justify": "left",
                                    "style": None,
                                    "no_wrap": False,
                                    "overflow": "fold"})
  assert recorded[2] == ("Who", {"justify": "left",
                                 "style": None,
                                 "no_wrap": False,
                                 "overflow": "ellipsis"})
@
